  "rule": "Find the hub: a 2x2 background hole with a two-cell bar of a distinct colour on each side. Erase all loose shapes. For each loose shape whose colour matches a bar, rotate it (no reflection) so that its centred two-cell tip faces that bar. Place it just outside the bar, with the tip aligned to the bar's two cells. Shapes of non-matching colours vanish.",
  "scope": "Supported: grid sizes 15..30 per side, random hub position, four distinct random bar colours, 1-4 docking shapes on random sides, 0-2 distractor shapes of non-hub colours, random input rotations, random tip-down shapes (widths 2/4/6, heights 2-5, symmetric or not). Not supported: a background other than 8 by default (it can be passed explicitly), several shapes of the same hub colour, reflected shapes, hub sides with repeated colours, and docked shapes that overlap or leave the grid.",
  "constraints": [
    "Exactly one hub-like site exists in the input (checked locally), and it is the true hub.",
    "All four bar colours are distinct and differ from the background.",
    "At most one loose shape per hub colour; distractor colours are distinct and not hub colours.",
    "Every shape is a single 8-connected component, has a tight bounding box and at least 4 cells.",
    "Only one rotation of a shape places a centred two-cell tip on the facing edge, so docking is unambiguous.",
    "Loose shapes keep a one-cell (8-neighbour) gap from each other and from the hub's 4x4 box.",
    "Docked shapes do not overlap each other or the hub, and fit inside the grid.",
    "Every sample contains at least one docking shape, so the output always differs from the input."
  ],
